    "body": "Fixes #592.\n\nAfter opening a second tab, `vim.windows` currently includes windows from both tabs. This differs from Vim's Python interface and can make plugins act on windows outside the current tab.\n\nUse `nvim_tabpage_list_wins(0)` for the remote sequence. Resolving tabpage 0 on each request also makes a saved reference to `vim.windows` follow tab switches and tab closure. Explicit `tabpage.windows` remains bound to its own tab. `RemoteSequence` accepts optional request arguments to support this without adding an extra round trip.\n\nThe regression exercises a real embedded Neovim instance, including iteration, indexing, slicing, membership, switching and closing tabs. Existing cross-tab tests now address the intended tab explicitly. The documentation points callers that need every tab's windows to `vim.api.list_wins()`.\n\nValidation on Linux, Python 3.12, Neovim 0.12.5:\n- New regression fails on unchanged upstream code and passes with the fix.\n- Full pytest suite: 81 passed.\n- Full flake8 and mypy checks pass (39 source files).\n- Sphinx HTML documentation build passes.\n\nThis changes the previously incorrect all-tabs behavior of `vim.windows`; callers intentionally depending on that behavior should use `vim.api.list_wins()`. Windows/macOS and Neovim's separate upstream test suite were not run locally.\n\nPrepared with AI assistance; the change and test results were checked against the repository.\n",
